      "kind": "think",
      "args": {
        "note": "Write flow for RST: BaseReader.write -> write_header (FixedWidthHeader.write is a no-op/pass) -> FixedWidthData.write. FixedWidthData.write appends, in order: (a) one line per entry in self.data.header_rows, (b) the position line (====) if header.position_line is not None, (c) data lines. So after super().write, lines = [hdr0, hdr1, ..., hdrN-1, position(N), data...]. RST.write currently bookends with lines[1], which only works for N=1 (header_rows=[\"name\"]). Fix: (1) RST.__init__(self, header_rows=None) forwarding to FixedWidth.__init__ (which already handles header_rows and sets self.data.header_rows); (2) RST.write bookend index = len(self.data.header_rows). This makes N=1 identical to before (regression-safe) and N>1 correct. Read path: existing RST read tests use default header_rows (no header_rows kwarg) so unaffected; SimpleRSTData.start_line=3 stays since FixedWidth only sets it when None. Task is about OUTPUT (write) per the example (tbl.write). I'll implement write support only, keep it minimal. Delegate the precise 2-line change to a minion with verdict checks (reproduction + existing test suite)."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "delegate_to_minion",
      "args": {
        "description": "Add header_rows support to the RST table writer so that writing a table with format=\"ascii.rst\", header_rows=[...] works instead of raising TypeError. Make exactly these two edits in /testbed/astropy/io/ascii/rst.py and change nothing else:\n\n1) In the RST class, change the __init__ method from:\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__(delimiter_pad=None, bookend=False)\nto:\n    def __init__(self, header_rows=None):\n        super().__init__(delimiter_pad=None, bookend=False, header_rows=header_rows)\n\n2) In the RST class, change the write method from:\n    def write(self, lines):\n        lines = super().write(lines)\n        lines = [lines[1]] + lines + [lines[1]]\n        return lines\nto:\n    def write(self, lines):\n        lines = super().write(lines)\n        position_line = lines[len(self.data.header_rows)]\n        lines = [position_line] + lines + [position_line]\n        return lines\n\nContext for why: FixedWidth.__init__ (already exists) normalizes header_rows (default [\"name\"]) and stores it on self.data.header_rows. FixedWidthData.write emits one line per header_rows entry, then the '===' position line, then data \u2014 so the position line is at index len(header_rows). The existing default (header_rows=[\"name\"]) must produce byte-identical output to before. Do NOT modify fixedwidth.py, core.py, or any test file. Do NOT add read support.",
